(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-6-copd-quiz"]={"title":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 6","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 6","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"In a patient with a post-bronchodilator ratio below 0.70, a forced expiratory volume in 1 second of 42% predicted corresponds to which airflow grade?","choices":["Grade 1 (mild)","Grade 2 (moderate)","Grade 3 (severe)","Grade 4 (very severe)"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Grade 3, severe, covers a post-bronchodilator 1-second volume of 30% up to 50% predicted, so 42% falls there. Grade 2 starts at 50% and grade 4 is below 30%. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which statement about albuterol in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is accurate?","choices":["It replaces long-acting therapy","Every patient should have one","It is reserved for severe disease","Frequent use is harmless"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every patient should have an albuterol inhaler to prevent or reduce symptoms, as needed. It should not be used excessively, because of toxicity and the risk of cardiac and psychiatric side effects. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"An acute exacerbation is defined as worsening dyspnea, cough or sputum developing over how many days or fewer?","choices":["3 days","7 days","14 days","30 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An acute exacerbation is an acute worsening of dyspnea, cough or sputum over 14 days or less, triggered by viral or bacterial infection or pollution. Pneumonia, pulmonary embolism, acute heart failure and pneumothorax must be excluded. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the primary risk factor for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Sedentary lifestyle","Tobacco smoking or smoke exposure","Burning biomass for cooking","Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tobacco smoking or exposure is the primary risk factor, behind about 90% of cases. Secondary risks are biomass burning, occupational dusts and fumes, a sedentary lifestyle and, much more rarely, alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which adverse effect is the main one for long-acting antimuscarinic antagonists such as tiotropium?","choices":["Dry mouth","Tachyarrhythmias","Pneumonia","Weight gain and bruising"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Long-acting antimuscarinics block M3 receptors to limit bronchoconstriction and help prevent exacerbations, with mild help in reducing sputum. Absorption is poor, so adverse effects are limited, mainly to dry mouth. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which test result separates emphysema from chronic bronchitis and asthma?","choices":["Raised serum bicarbonate","Reduced diffusing capacity","Ratio below 0.70","Bronchodilator response under 12%"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The diffusing capacity is reduced in emphysema, because destroyed alveoli and bullae limit gas transfer, separating it from chronic bronchitis (normal or near normal) and asthma. Spirometry alone cannot tell emphysema from chronic bronchitis. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 28"},{"q":"Which finding is one of the discharge criteria after an exacerbation?","choices":["Albuterol no more than every 4 hours","Normal spirometry","Climbing a full flight of stairs","Stable for at least 72 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Discharge criteria include needing inhaled short-acting beta-2 agonist no more often than every 4 hours, using long-acting bronchodilators, walking across the room if previously ambulatory, eating and sleeping without frequent waking, and clinical stability for 12 to 24 hours. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is noted about pulmonary rehabilitation referral for older adults?","choices":["It serves just a return to work","They are referred less, so refer actively","Rehabilitation is unsafe past 65","Older adults need no rehabilitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Older adults are less often referred for rehabilitation, so the advice is to refer them actively. For younger adults, rehabilitation aims at a return to work and activity. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the main role of the chest x-ray in this disease?","choices":["Confirming the diagnosis","Grading airflow obstruction","Measuring air trapping","Ruling out other diagnoses"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The chest x-ray is not a diagnostic standard, but it can rule out other diagnoses such as heart failure or pneumothorax and may show overinflated hyperlucent lungs, a flattened diaphragm, bullae or a narrow vertical heart. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"In the Five A's approach to behavior change, which step comes right after Advise?","choices":["Assist with a plan","Ask about the behavior","Assess readiness to change","Arrange follow-up"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The Five A's run Ask (open a nonjudgmental conversation), Advise (clear, tailored advice), Assess (readiness and willingness to change), Assist (realistic steps and strategies) and Arrange (follow-up to monitor progress). — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Long-term supplemental oxygen is indicated when the resting arterial oxygen pressure falls below which value?","choices":["55 mmHg","65 mmHg","75 mmHg","85 mmHg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Supplemental oxygen is indicated when the arterial oxygen pressure is below 55 mmHg or the saturation below 88%, and it is proven to decrease the risk of death, prolong life and improve quality of life. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Which care priority is listed for adults aged about 40 to 64 rather than for older adults?","choices":["Staying in work","Goals of care","Managing multimorbidity","Quality of life"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For adults about 40 to 64 the priorities are smoking cessation, preserving lung function, staying in work and preventing exacerbations. For adults 65 and older they shift to function, quality of life, multimorbidity and goals of care. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which measurement is one of the 4 inputs of the BODE (body mass index, obstruction, dyspnea, exercise) index?","choices":["6-minute walk distance","Arterial carbon dioxide pressure","Diffusing capacity","Symptom assessment test score"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The index scores body mass index, airflow obstruction (1-second volume, percent predicted), dyspnea (the modified Medical Research Council grade) and exercise capacity, measured as the 6-minute walk distance, to estimate 4-year survival. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Under the current three-group scheme, a patient at high exacerbation risk is placed in which group regardless of symptoms?","choices":["Group B","Group D","Group E","Group A"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the 2023/2026 scheme, low-risk patients are group A or B by symptoms, and any high-risk patient (2 or more moderate exacerbations or 1 hospitalization) is group E, which combines the old groups C and D. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 40, 41"},{"q":"Which feature belongs to the chronic bronchitis subtype rather than emphysema?","choices":["Earlier hypoxemia and hypercapnia","Gas exchange preserved until late","Reduced diffusing capacity","Hyperinflation with bullae on imaging"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the chronic bronchitis subtype, airway narrowing and mucus plugging bring earlier hypoxemia and hypercapnia, with cough, sputum, rhonchi and possible cyanosis and edema. In emphysema, gas exchange is relatively preserved until late. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Why is a complete blood count part of the workup for this disease?","choices":["To measure alpha-1 antitrypsin","To look for heart failure","To find anemia and count eosinophils","To confirm the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The complete blood count checks for coincident anemia and measures eosinophils; an elevated eosinophil count is when adding an inhaled corticosteroid is considered. Anemia is also on the comorbidity screening list. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 61"},{"q":"Which test is required to establish the diagnosis of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Arterial blood gas","Spirometry with a bronchodilator","Chest x-ray","Computed tomography of the chest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Spirometry, the cornerstone test, is required to establish the diagnosis and is done before and after a bronchodilator such as albuterol. A post-bronchodilator ratio of forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity below 0.70 confirms it. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 28, 30, 31"},{"q":"In the emphysema subtype, what causes the airways to obstruct?","choices":["Mucus plugging of narrowed airways","Allergen-triggered reversible bronchospasm","Stiff chest wall limiting inspiration","Lost elastic recoil collapsing airways"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls, and the loss of elastic recoil lets the airways collapse on expiration, trapping air. In chronic bronchitis, obstruction instead comes from airway narrowing and mucus plugging. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 25"},{"q":"Which laboratory finding prompts consideration of adding an inhaled corticosteroid?","choices":["Low alpha-1 antitrypsin","Raised natriuretic peptide","Raised blood eosinophils","Raised serum bicarbonate"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For group E, a long-acting beta agonist plus antimuscarinic is the start, with an inhaled corticosteroid considered when blood eosinophils are 300 or more; at discharge after an exacerbation, an inhaled corticosteroid is considered if eosinophils are elevated. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 61"},{"q":"Next to smoking cessation, which therapy tends to be the most effective management for chronic obstructive pulmonary disease?","choices":["Oral corticosteroids","Pulmonary rehabilitation","Inhaled corticosteroids","Long-term azithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Next to smoking cessation, pulmonary rehabilitation tends to be the most effective therapy, reducing dyspnea, improving exercise tolerance and mobility, and reducing hospitalizations and readmissions. It needs a healthcare professional or team. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Smoking-related emphysema typically follows which pattern?","choices":["Panlobular, lower lobes","Bronchial wall thickening","Diffuse fibrosis at the bases","Centrilobular, upper lobes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema from smoking is centrilobular and predominantly in the upper lobes. The panlobular, lower-lobe pattern belongs to alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency, which is why basilar emphysema prompts testing for it. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A patient's disease is severe, with a forced expiratory volume in 1 second below 40% predicted. When should the pneumococcal vaccine be given?","choices":["Not until age 65","During the next exacerbation","After age 75","When the severity is recognized"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Every patient should receive the pneumococcal vaccine at age 65, but those with severe disease (1-second volume below 40%) or cardiac comorbidities should get it earlier, essentially when diagnosed and recognized at that severity. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which body habitus statement about patients with this disease is accurate?","choices":["Weight gain signals recovery","Many gain weight from inactivity","They are thin in every case","Obesity rules out emphysema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients are not always thin; they may be overweight, obese or gaining weight because breathlessness keeps them inactive. The thin pursed-lip breather is a classic emphysema picture, not a rule for everyone. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which lung volume change reflects air trapping in this disease?","choices":["Reduced residual volume","Reduced total lung capacity","Reduced functional residual capacity","Raised residual volume"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Air trapping raises the residual volume, and hyperinflation raises total lung capacity and functional residual capacity. With exertion, dynamic hyperinflation worsens and drives dyspnea. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Spirometry shows obstruction and the forced vital capacity is also low, raising the possibility of a mixed disorder. Which measurement confirms it?","choices":["Diffusing capacity","A repeat bronchodilator response","Arterial blood gas","Total lung capacity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When an obstructed study also has a low forced vital capacity, a mixed obstructive and restrictive disorder is possible and is confirmed with total lung capacity. Lung volumes identify hyperinflation or coexisting restriction. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 27, 28"},{"q":"Which statement about sputum production in this disease is accurate?","choices":["It reflects chronic bronchitis","It is present in every patient","It is typical of pure emphysema","Its absence rules out the disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients do not always produce sputum: it is seen with chronic bronchitis or a combined presentation and is typically not prevalent in emphysema-only cases. Its absence does not exclude the disease. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Where can most exacerbations of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease be managed?","choices":["In the intensive care unit","On a hospital ward","In pulmonary rehabilitation","As outpatients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. More than 80% of exacerbations can be managed as outpatients after triage of severity. Admission is reserved for marked symptoms despite acute management, need for noninvasive ventilation, new signs, arrhythmia, poor secretion control, comorbidities or lack of home support. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which emphysema pattern does alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency cause?","choices":["Bronchial wall thickening","Isolated large bullae in the apex","Panlobular, lower lobes","Centrilobular, upper lobes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency causes panlobular emphysema predominantly in the lower lobes, which shows as basilar emphysema on the chest x-ray. Smoking causes centrilobular emphysema in the upper lobes. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 19"},{"q":"How do bullae differ from blebs?","choices":["Bullae occur in asthma, blebs in emphysema","Bullae lie deeper and grow larger","Bullae lie under the pleura and stay small","Bullae are filled with fluid"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blebs arise from destroyed airspaces and are not contained by the connective tissue of the visceral pleura, while bullae develop deep to the internal elastic layer, form their own sacs within connective tissue, and become significantly bigger than blebs. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 7, 33"},{"q":"Under the current three-group scheme, which initial pharmacologic treatment fits group B?","choices":["A single bronchodilator","An inhaled corticosteroid alone","Daily oral corticosteroids","Dual long-acting bronchodilators"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Group B, more symptoms (dyspnea grade 2 or more, or assessment score 10 or more) with low exacerbation risk, starts on a long-acting beta agonist plus a long-acting antimuscarinic. A single combined inhaler may be more convenient and effective. — 37. svCOPD 2026.pptx, Slides 37, 40"}]};
